 "response": "Bitron's budget is now **$2,533K**. I reached this by working through the log by hand, so a small arithmetic slip is possible.\n\n**Method**\n- Only Finance Office budget entries count. Messages from other teams are ignored.\n- Finance Office entries that don't change the amount (cost-centre codes, receipts, audits, room moves) are also ignored.\n- The starting point is #0001, which sets the budget to $2,470K.\n\n**Withdrawals**\n- Where one withdrawal cancels another, I followed the chain back to the original update. Each withdrawal in a chain flips the result, so an update ends up withdrawn if an odd number of withdrawals act on it.\n  - Example: 851 \u2192 794 \u2192 362 \u2192 149 \u2192 18 \u2192 8 \u2192 4 \u2192 3. #0851 stands, so #0794 is cancelled, so #0362 stands, and so on. The result is that #0003 (\u2212$441K) is withdrawn.\n  - Example: #0667 cancels #0036, so #0017 (+$67K) still applies.\n- In total, about 130 updates end up withdrawn, including #0010, #0024, #0055, #0359, #0561, #0834 and #0918.\n- Updates restored by a second-level withdrawal still apply, for example:\n  - #0026 (+$394K)\n  - #0143 (\u2212$478K)\n  - #0320 (\u2212$596K)\n  - #0509 (\u2212$529K)\n  - #1054 (+$537K)\n  - #1318 (\u2212$474K)\n  - #1358 (\u2212$476K)\n\n**Result**\nApplying every surviving increase and decrease in order from $2,470K gives a final budget of $2,533K.\n\nANSWER: $2533K",
